Original commit body from 523509fa230f5c9bdf36e22ca2059fefb89ce2e9:

WI-razib / INV-kodan. The pyffi linker had no test-file awareness, and on
the self-corpus that made its entire observable output its own fixtures:
all 10 C_stdlib FFI `calls` edges (fopen/fwrite/popen/...) originated from
test_pyffi_linker.py, because no production code here uses ctypes or cffi.
Phantom edges in the graph, plus (per INV-kodan) seconds of CPU per analysis
spent validating the pass against itself.

The fix is the convention ~22 sibling linkers already follow: the shared
paths.is_test_file chokepoint. Worth recording is why pyffi could NOT simply
adopt discovery.find_non_test_files as the 2026-06-27 linker-evidence-gating
sweep did for its siblings — pyffi never walks the repo, it consumes
in-memory symbol lists, so the "non-adopter" framing does not fit it and the
gate belongs on the symbol path instead.

Both emit phases are covered, not just the one the filing measured:
  Phase 1 (ctypes/cffi file scan) filters the scanned path set.
  Phase 2 (PyO3) never sees a path — it walks existing edges keyed on
    edge.dst — so it matches edge.src against the ids of Python symbols
    that live in test files.
Gating only Phase 1 would have closed the measured half and left the same
defect open on the other.

Each phase has a test, paired with a positive control asserting production
ctypes calls still link, so a too-broad gate that silently deleted the
linker's real output could not pass as a fix.

Behavioral evidence (fresh self-survey, 40979 nodes / 138626 edges): the
pyffi-linker AnalysisRun still executes (2116ms) and now emits 0 edges,
down from the filed 10 — the pass ran, so the zero is not vacuous.

